# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Change-aware manuscript, part 4: Section 7 (Case study II: scaffold structural monitoring) and Section 8 (cross-domain reliability and ablation)."""
HD("7. Case study II: scaffold structural monitoring")
HD("7.1 The synthetic scaffold benchmark", 2)
P(f"A scaffold of {DS['N']} members in {DS['N'] // DS['bay']} bays is monitored for {W6.T_EPOCHS} days. Members are removed or disconnected by authorized work (about {DS['lam_A'] * DS['N'] * W6.T_EPOCHS:.1f} events per scaffold over the horizon, leaving a hazard in {int(100 * DS['theta_A'])}% of the cases because the work does not always include compensating measures) "
  f"and by unauthorized removal ({DS['lam_U'] * DS['N'] * W6.T_EPOCHS:.1f} events, a hazard in {int(100 * DS['theta_U'])}% of the cases). Point sensors (tilt and clamp proximity) report an event with probability {DS['d_e']}; a vision sweep every {W6.DELTA} days reads each visible member; {int(100 * DS['occl'])}% of the members are occluded by tarps or netting and are unobserved. "
  f"The structure is unsafe if a critical member, or two non-critical members of one bay, are hazardous; in the assumed rates a scaffold is unsafe in {TR(SF,'SSI','cond_risk',0)}% of the scaffold-days of the test worlds. "
  "The criterion and the rates are assumptions that make the benchmark simple and monotone; they are not engineering claims, and a defensible analysis would need hazard labels from a structural model or an independent assessment.", align="justify")
HD("7.2 Dynamic changes and observation staleness (E1, E2)", 2)
r1s, a1s, b1s = ratio_at(SF, "S0", "D2", 0.30, "dynamic_vs_static")
r2s, a2s, b2s = H1_SF
P(f"At the structure level the static twin continues {E1(SF,'S0','coverage',0,level='structure')}% of the scaffold-days at a conditional risk of {E1(SF,'S0','cond_risk',1,level='structure')}%; the dynamic estimator with work permits as context continues {E1(SF,'D2','coverage',0,level='structure')}% at {E1(SF,'D2','cond_risk',1,level='structure')}%. "
  f"The two are not at the same operating point, so the comparison is made at matched coverage: at 30% the static twin has {r1s:.1f} times and static-state fusion {r2s:.1f} times the conditional risk of D2 ({a2s*100:.1f}% against {b2s*100:.1f}%), and the advantage persists but narrows toward full coverage "
  f"(at 90%: {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['S1'],'s_curve',0.9),1)}% for static-state fusion against {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['D2'],'s_curve',0.9),1)}%). The static twin misses {m6(DY[SF]['methods']['S0']['delay']['share_missed'],0)}% of the hazard onsets and the dynamic estimator {m6(DY[SF]['methods']['D2']['delay']['share_missed'],0)}%, "
  f"with mean delays of {m6(DY[SF]['methods']['S0']['delay']['mean_delay'],1,1)} and {m6(DY[SF]['methods']['D2']['delay']['mean_delay'],1,1)} days. At the element level the risk of the static twin grows from {AGE(SF,'S0','cond_risk',0,3)}% just after a sweep to {AGE(SF,'S0','cond_risk',11,3)}% eleven days later and that of D2 from {AGE(SF,'D2','cond_risk',0,3)}% to {AGE(SF,'D2','cond_risk',11,3)}%, "
  f"and the dynamic estimator again predicts its own risk ({AGE(SF,'D2','predicted',11,3)}% at eleven days; Fig. 5b). H1 is supported in this domain at matched coverage.", align="justify")
HD("7.3 Permit reconciliation (E3)", 2)
P(f"Among removals made under a valid permit the suppressing rule flags {PE(SF,'D1','change_valid_permit','flagged')}% of the elements and leaves {PE(SF,'D1','change_valid_permit','hazard_missed',1)}% hazardous and unflagged, against {PE(SF,'D0','change_valid_permit','flagged')}% flagged and {PE(SF,'D0','change_valid_permit','hazard_missed',1)}% missed without work records and "
  f"{PE(SF,'D2','change_valid_permit','flagged')}% flagged and {PE(SF,'D2','change_valid_permit','hazard_missed',1)}% missed with the permit as context (Fig. 6d–f). Over all hazard onsets the suppressing rule misses {m6(PERM[SF]['D1']['delay']['share_missed'],0)}%, no records {m6(PERM[SF]['D0']['delay']['share_missed'],0)}% and context {m6(PERM[SF]['D2']['delay']['share_missed'],0)}%. "
  f"The context estimator classifies the reported changes it decides on correctly in {PEA(SF,'D2','accuracy_when_decided',1)}% of the cases and leaves {PEA(SF,'D2','uncertain')}% uncertain; without records the best available classification is the prior share of authorized events ({PEA(SF,'D0','accuracy_when_decided',0)}%). "
  "The false-escalation rate is below 0.1% for all three, because the sensors and the vision have low false-alarm probabilities in this domain. H2 is supported: the records as context improved attribution and did not increase the undetected hazardous states, whereas suppression multiplied them.", align="justify")
HD("7.4 Hybrid sensing and placement (E4, E5)", 2)
P(f"Point sensors on 5% to 20% of the members, with only the baseline sweep, miss {HYD(SF,'point_only_5','share_missed',d=0)}% to {HYD(SF,'point_only_20','share_missed',d=0)}% of the hazard onsets, because most members have no sensor; sweeps every 12 days alone miss {HYD(SF,'survey_only_12','share_missed',d=0)}% (mean delay {HYD(SF,'survey_only_12','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} days), "
  f"and the hybrid with sensors on 10% of the members {HYD(SF,'hybrid_10_12','share_missed',d=0)}% ({HYD(SF,'hybrid_10_12','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} days); sweeps every 6 days miss {HYD(SF,'survey_only_6','share_missed',d=0)}% ({HYD(SF,'survey_only_6','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} days) at a cost of {R6['hybrid'][SF]['survey_only_6']['cost']:.0f} node equivalents against {R6['hybrid'][SF]['hybrid_10_12']['cost']:.0f} for the hybrid (Fig. 7b, d). "
  "As in the utility problem, the continuous layer helped less than the cadence of the survey, and its benefit is limited by how few elements it observes; the structure-level conditional risks of the configurations differ by less than their intervals (Supplementary Table S9). "
  f"Allocation matters more here: at a budget of 10% the unsafe-decision probability of D2 is {PL(SF,'0.1','random','unsafe_prob',3)}% with random, {PL(SF,'0.1','uniform','unsafe_prob',3)}% with uniform, {PL(SF,'0.1','uncertainty','unsafe_prob',3)}% with uncertainty-based and {PL(SF,'0.1','risk_optimised','unsafe_prob',3)}% with risk-optimized placement, and the share of hazard onsets missed "
  f"is {PLD(SF,'0.1','random','share_missed')}%, {PLD(SF,'0.1','uniform','share_missed')}%, {PLD(SF,'0.1','uncertainty','share_missed')}% and {PLD(SF,'0.1','risk_optimised','share_missed')}% (Fig. 8b). Placing sensors where the vision is blind (the occluded bays) was the best strategy; the risk-optimized rule, which learns the importance-weighted unsafe mass from training worlds, was no better than uniform spacing at 10% and better only at 20% "
  f"({PL(SF,'0.2','risk_optimised','unsafe_prob',3)}% against {PL(SF,'0.2','uniform','unsafe_prob',3)}%). A reduction of modelled risk at a fixed budget is therefore obtainable, but the simple uncertainty-based rule obtained more of it than the more elaborate one; the pilot worlds of 40 realizations per site may be too few to rank the members reliably.", align="justify")
HD("7.5 Domain-specific baselines (E6)", 2)
rs, ras, rbs = ratio_at(SF, "SSI", "D2", 0.30)
r5s, ra5, rb5 = ratio_at(SF, "SSI", "D2", 0.50)
P(f"A point-sensor alarm that escalates a structure once any healthy sensor has reported a change continues {TR(SF,'ANY','coverage',0)}% of the scaffold-days at a conditional risk of {TR(SF,'ANY','cond_risk',1)}% and escalates {TR(SF,'ANY','false_escalation_rate',0)}% of the safe scaffold-days, because sensors have false-event probabilities and the structure has {DS['N']} members. "
  f"A weighted index of importance-weighted event reports and survey detections, of the kind used in operational displays, ranks the scaffold-days at a conditional risk of {ras*100:.1f}% at 30% coverage and {ra5*100:.1f}% at 50%, against {rbs*100:.1f}% and {rb5*100:.1f}% for D2 (Fig. 9b); "
  f"suppressing permit-matched changes did not change its ranking noticeably ({m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['SSI'],'s_curve',0.3),1)}% against {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['SSI_NS'],'s_curve',0.3),1)}% at 30%), probably because in these worlds the index is dominated by the survey false alarms and the few events; the damage of suppression is in the hazard states it hides (Section 7.3), which a risk–coverage curve of the index does not show. "
  "The index is a display, not a probability: its value has no calibrated meaning as a hazard probability, and the comparison is of its ranking only.", align="justify")

# =============================================================================================== 8 Cross-domain
HD("8. Cross-domain reliability, combined failures and ablation")
HD("8.1 Matched-coverage comparison with domain-specific baselines (E6)", 2)
rows = [["Domain, decision level", "Method", "Risk at 10%", "20%", "30%", "50%", "70%"]]
for dom, lab in ((UT, "Utilities, element"), (SF, "Scaffold, structure")):
    key, _ = lvl(dom)
    for m in (("S0", "S1", "D2", "G", "ORACLE") if dom == UT else ("S0", "S1", "SSI", "D2", "G", "ORACLE")):
        rows.append([lab, W6.METHOD_LABEL.get(m, W6.BASELINE_LABEL.get(m, m))] + [m6(cvx(R6["transfer"][dom][m], key, c), 2) for c in (0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7)])
H.caption(doc, "**Table 5.** Conditional risk (%) at matched decision coverage (10% to 70%) for the static, domain-specific, dynamic and gated methods, with point sensors on 10% of the elements (means over test sites; intervals in the Supplementary Material).", keep_next=True)
H.table(doc, rows, widths=[1.5, 2.6, 0.55, 0.45, 0.45, 0.45, 0.45], size=7.2)
P(f"In both domains the dynamic estimator has a lower conditional risk than the static methods at every matched coverage from 10% to 50% (in utilities the intervals overlap at 70%, where the risk of every method approaches the prevalence) (utilities: {ratio_at(UT,'S1','D2',0.2)[0]:.1f} times lower at 20%; scaffold: {ratio_at(SF,'S1','D2',0.2)[0]:.1f} times at 20%), and than the weighted index at 30% in the scaffold domain ({rs:.1f} times). "
  f"The gated estimator ranks slightly worse than D2 at matched coverage (utilities: {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][UT]['G'],'curve',0.3),2)}% against {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][UT]['D2'],'curve',0.3),2)}% at 30%; scaffold: {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['G'],'s_curve',0.3),2)}% against {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['D2'],'s_curve',0.3),2)}%), so that its value is not a better ranking but a different operating point, as the next section shows. H3 is supported in the narrow sense stated: a common architecture gave a measurable benefit in two structurally different problems against calibrated, domain-specific baselines, in simulation, with simple change processes and independent elements.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig36_transfer.png"), 6.5, "**Fig. 9.** Cross-domain comparison at matched decision coverage: conditional risk against coverage for the static, dynamic, gated and oracle methods in (a) utilities (element level) and (b) the scaffold (structure level), with the weighted index (ranking) and the point-sensor alarm (single point) in the scaffold domain. Point sensors on 10% of the elements; shaded bands are 95% intervals over test sites.", alt="Transfer")
HD("8.2 Combined failures and the reliability gate (E7)", 2)
rows = [["Domain", "Scenario", "Static fusion", "No records", "Context (D2)", "Gated", "Oracle"]]
for dom, lab in ((UT, "Utilities"), (SF, "Scaffold")):
    for sc in W6.SCENARIOS:
        rows.append([lab, W6.SCEN_LABEL[sc]] + ["%s (%s)" % (CB(dom, sc, m, "cond_risk", 1), CB(dom, sc, m, "coverage", 0)) for m in ("S1", "D0", "D2", "G", "ORACLE")])
H.caption(doc, "**Table 6.** Conditional risk (%) and, in brackets, decision coverage (%) under single and combined failures, with point sensors on 10% of the elements. The estimators are calibrated on the nominal world and are not recalibrated. A coverage below 1% is shown to one decimal; a conditional risk is undefined, and shown as n/a, only where no decision is released.", keep_next=True)
H.table(doc, rows, widths=[0.8, 2.0, 0.85, 0.85, 0.9, 0.85, 0.8], size=7.0)
cu, covu = CBr(UT, "combined", "D2", "G", 0)
cs, covs = CBr(SF, "combined", "D2", "G", 0)
eu, coveu = CBr(UT, "event_rate_and_bursts", "D2", "G", 0)
es, coves = CBr(SF, "event_rate_and_bursts", "D2", "G", 0)
wu, covwu = CBr(UT, "weak_survey", "D2", "G", 0)
P(f"Table 6 and Fig. 10 give the conditional risk and the coverage under each failure. Unflagged sensor failures, erroneous work records and a weak survey are failure modes of different kinds. "
  f"Unflagged failures of the point sensors change little ({CB(UT,'silent_sensor_failures','D2','cond_risk',1)}% against {CB(UT,'nominal','D2','cond_risk',1)}% for D2 in utilities), because the sensors observe few elements. Erroneous records raise the risk of the context estimator toward that of the estimator without records ({CB(UT,'erroneous_permits','D2','cond_risk',1)}% against {CB(UT,'erroneous_permits','D0','cond_risk',1)}% in utilities). "
  f"A change process three times as frequent as calibrated and arriving in bursts raises the risk of D2 from {CB(UT,'nominal','D2','cond_risk',1)}% to {CB(UT,'event_rate_and_bursts','D2','cond_risk',1)}% (utilities) and from {CB(SF,'nominal','D2','cond_risk',1)}% to {CB(SF,'event_rate_and_bursts','D2','cond_risk',1)}% (scaffold); "
  f"a survey weaker than assumed to {CB(UT,'weak_survey','D2','cond_risk',1)}% (utilities), because the estimator trusts silence from a survey that is no longer as informative. All the failures together give {CB(UT,'combined','D2','cond_risk',1)}% and {CB(SF,'combined','D2','cond_risk',1)}%.", align="justify")
P(f"The gate lowers the risk under these departures: {CB(UT,'weak_survey','G','cond_risk',1)}% against {CB(UT,'weak_survey','D2','cond_risk',1)}% under a weak survey in utilities, {CB(SF,'combined','G','cond_risk',1)}% against {CB(SF,'combined','D2','cond_risk',1)}% in the combined scaffold scenario. "
  f"But it does so by abstaining: its coverage is {CB(UT,'weak_survey','G','coverage',0)}% against {CB(UT,'weak_survey','D2','coverage',0)}% under the weak survey, {CB(SF,'combined','G','coverage',0)}% against {CB(SF,'combined','D2','coverage',0)}% in the combined scaffold scenario, and {CB(UT,'combined','G','coverage',1)}% of the decisions in the combined utility scenario. "
  f"At the coverage of the gate, D2 has a risk of {eu*100:.1f}% against the gate’s {CB(UT,'event_rate_and_bursts','G','cond_risk',1)}% under the faster change process in utilities (gate coverage {coveu*100:.0f}%), and {es*100:.1f}% against {CB(SF,'event_rate_and_bursts','G','cond_risk',1)}% in the scaffold domain (coverage {coves*100:.0f}%); "
  f"in the combined scaffold scenario D2 at the gate’s coverage has {cs*100:.1f}% against the gate’s {CB(SF,'combined','G','cond_risk',1)}%. The oracle, which knows the true event statistics and the sensor health, is not safe either under the combined failure in utilities ({CB(UT,'combined','ORACLE','cond_risk',1)}%), "
  "where the survey is the weak link. The gate thus converts a mismatch it can detect from the surveys into abstention. It cannot protect against a failure the surveys do not reveal, and the abstention has a cost in decisions that cannot be taken.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig37_combined_failures.png"), 6.5, "**Fig. 10.** Combined failures: conditional risk (a, b) and decision coverage (c, d) of static fusion, the dynamic estimators, the reliability gate and the oracle under the six scenarios of Table 6, for utilities (left) and the scaffold (right; structure level). The estimators are not recalibrated.", alt="Combined failures")
HD("8.3 Ablation", 2)
P(f"Removing one component of the gated method at a time (Fig. 11) shows what each does. Without the dynamics (static-state fusion) the nominal risk rises from {AB(UT,'nominal','full','cond_risk',1)}% to {AB(UT,'nominal','no_dynamics','cond_risk',1)}% in utilities and from {AB(SF,'nominal','full','cond_risk',1)}% to {AB(SF,'nominal','no_dynamics','cond_risk',1)}% in the scaffold (but the coverage rises from {AB(UT,'nominal','full','coverage',0)}% to {AB(UT,'nominal','no_dynamics','coverage',0)}% and from {AB(SF,'nominal','full','coverage',0)}% to {AB(SF,'nominal','no_dynamics','coverage',0)}%); "
  f"without the permit context the risk rises to {AB(UT,'nominal','no_permit_context','cond_risk',1)}% and {AB(SF,'nominal','no_permit_context','cond_risk',1)}%; without the survey the scaffold risk rises to {AB(SF,'nominal','no_survey','cond_risk',1)}% and the utility corridor cannot be released at all, since the baseline hazard prevalence is unknown without a survey. "
  f"Without the point sensors the risk is {AB(UT,'nominal','no_point_sensors','cond_risk',1)}% and {AB(SF,'nominal','no_point_sensors','cond_risk',1)}%, close to the {AB(UT,'nominal','full','cond_risk',1)}% and {AB(SF,'nominal','full','cond_risk',1)}% with them, and without the health flags {AB(UT,'nominal','no_health_flags','cond_risk',1)}% and {AB(SF,'nominal','no_health_flags','cond_risk',1)}%, which confirms that in these worlds the point sensors and their health matter little. "
  f"Without the validity selection the gate takes the supremum over all alternatives and releases {AB(UT,'nominal','no_gate_selection','coverage',0)}% of the utility corridor in the nominal world: the selection is what keeps the gate usable. Under the combined failure, removing the gate raises the conditional risk from {AB(UT,'combined','full','cond_risk',1)}% to {AB(UT,'combined','no_gate','cond_risk',1)}% (utilities) and from {AB(SF,'combined','full','cond_risk',1)}% to {AB(SF,'combined','no_gate','cond_risk',1)}% (scaffold), "
  f"with coverage rising from {AB(UT,'combined','full','coverage',0)}% to {AB(UT,'combined','no_gate','coverage',0)}% and from {AB(SF,'combined','full','coverage',0)}% to {AB(SF,'combined','no_gate','coverage',0)}%. "
  f"The conditional risk of the full method under the combined failure in utilities ({AB(UT,'combined','full','cond_risk',1)}%) is therefore a statistic of a very small coverage and not of an empty set: it is computed over a mean of {m6(R6['ablation'][UT]['combined']['full']['element']['release'],0,1.0)} released element-epochs per test site "
  f"({AB(UT,'combined','full','coverage',1)}% of the element-epochs), and it is defined in {m6(R6['ablation'][UT]['combined']['full']['element']['risk_defined'],0)}% of the sites. It describes the few decisions that the gate still takes and is not an operating point; "
  "where a method releases nothing in a site, its conditional risk in that site is undefined, is reported as n/a and is left out of the mean.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig38_ablation.png"), 6.3, "**Fig. 11.** Ablation: conditional risk of the gated method with one component removed, in the nominal and the combined-failure scenarios, for utilities (a) and the scaffold (b; structure level). The coverage of every variant is in Supplementary Table S10.", alt="Ablation")
HD("8.4 Sensitivity to the structural hazard definition (E8)", 2)
P(f"The scaffold hazard criterion is synthetic: a structure is unsafe if a critical member is hazardous or if two non-critical members of one bay are. The member-level estimators do not depend on this definition, but the structure-level decisions, the unsafe-state labels and the weighted-index baseline do, "
  "and a conclusion that held only for one arbitrary definition would say little. E8 therefore re-evaluates the scaffold experiments under sixteen definitions, crossing the share of critical members (5, 10, 20 and 35%; 20% is the benchmark) with the number of non-critical members of a bay whose hazard makes the structure unsafe (1, 2, 3 and 4; 2 is the benchmark). "
  f"The simulated worlds and the member hazard probabilities are the same under every definition, the critical members of a smaller share are a subset of those of a larger share, and the release criterion (*P*(unsafe) ≤ {W6.TAU_S:.2f}) is unchanged. The prevalence of unsafe structure-epochs in the nominal world ranges from {100*min(SDP):.1f}% to {100*max(SDP):.1f}% across the definitions ({100*SDP[SD_KEYS.index(SD_BENCH)]:.1f}% for the benchmark), so the definitions span benign and demanding benchmarks.", align="justify")
sn, sc_, wn, wc_ = SDN[("nominal", "S1")], SDN[("combined", "S1")], SDN[("nominal", "SSI")], SDN[("combined", "SSI")]
P(f"The dynamic estimator had a lower structure-level risk than static-state fusion at every matched coverage from 10% to 50% in {sn['n_dom']} of the {sn['n_eval']} definitions in the nominal world and in {sc_['n_dom']} of {sc_['n_eval']} under the combined failures (Fig. 12a,b). "
  f"The risk ratio at 30% coverage ranged from {sn['rmin']:.1f} to {sn['rmax']:.1f} (median {sn['rmed']:.1f}) in the nominal world and from {sc_['rmin']:.1f} to {sc_['rmax']:.1f} (median {sc_['rmed']:.1f}) under the combined failures. "
  f"It was smallest where one non-critical member made a bay unsafe (ratio {sd_ratio('nominal',0.20,1,'S1','D2'):.1f} nominal, {sd_ratio('combined',0.20,1,'S1','D2'):.1f} combined at the benchmark critical share), because the structure is then unsafe so often that the prevalence compresses every method toward it, and it was largest for a small share of critical members, where the decision depends on the bays and so on the evidence about many members at once. "
  f"The dynamic estimator also had a lower risk than the weighted index at matched coverage in {wn['n_dom']} of {wn['n_eval']} (nominal) and {wc_['n_dom']} of {wc_['n_eval']} (combined) definitions, with ratios at 30% of {wn['rmin']:.1f} to {wn['rmax']:.1f} and {wc_['rmin']:.1f} to {wc_['rmax']:.1f} (Fig. 12c). "
  f"Under the combined failures the reliability gate released fewer structures than D2 in {SDG['n_lower_cov']} of {SDG['n']} definitions (at most {100*SDG['cov_max']:.0f}% of the structure-epochs, and none when one non-critical member made a bay unsafe, Fig. 12d), and where it released any its conditional risk was lower than that of D2 in {SDG['n_lower_risk']} of {SDG['n_risk_def']}. "
  f"The benchmark cell is an independent replicate of the worlds of Section 8.1 (the same sites, new simulated worlds): its ratio of {sd_ratio('nominal',0.20,2,'S1','D2'):.1f} against {H1_SF[0]:.1f} there has the same direction, but the risk of D2 at 30% coverage is {m6(cvx(R6['structure_definitions']['nominal']['c0.20_k2']['methods']['D2'],'s_curve',0.3),1)}% there against {m6(cvx(R6['transfer'][SF]['D2'],'s_curve',0.3),1)}% in Section 8.1, which shows how variable a ratio of two small risks is between replicate sets of worlds (Supplementary Table S17); the ratios in this section should be read as orders of magnitude and not as point estimates. "
  "The conclusions on H1 and H3 for the scaffold therefore do not depend on the single benchmark definition within the range tested, and the benefit of the gate remains a benefit bought in abstention. The qualification is unchanged and is stated in Section 9.4: all sixteen definitions are monotone criteria of the same kind, none is a structural calculation, "
  "and the sensitivity analysis shows that the findings are not an artifact of one definition, not that any definition describes a real scaffold. The full results are in Supplementary Table S17.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig39_structure_definitions.png"), 6.2, "**Fig. 12.** Sensitivity of the scaffold conclusions to the structural hazard definition (E8): share of critical members (vertical axis) against the number of non-critical members of a bay that make it unsafe (horizontal axis); the outlined cell is the benchmark. (a) Ratio of the conditional risk of static-state fusion to that of the dynamic estimator at 30% matched coverage, nominal world; (b) the same under the combined failures; (c) the same for the weighted index; (d) share of structure-epochs released by the reliability gate under the combined failures. Point sensors on 10% of the members.", alt="Structure definitions")
HD("8.5 Summary of the hypotheses", 2)
H.caption(doc, "**Table 7.** Outcome of the hypothesis tests (simulation; conditional on the assumed environments).", keep_next=True)
H.table(doc, [["Hypothesis", "Outcome", "Evidence", "Qualification"],
              ["H1 dynamic model validity", "Supported in both domains", f"risk {H1_UT[0]:.1f} (utilities) and {H1_SF[0]:.1f} (scaffold) times lower than static-state fusion at 30% matched coverage; predicted risk follows observed risk with age", "advantage depends on event rate and survey interval; narrows at high coverage"],
              ["H2 context-aware attribution", "Supported", f"hazardous unflagged after valid-permit changes: suppression {m6(SUPP_MISS[UT],0)}% / {m6(SUPP_MISS[SF],0)}%, context {m6(CTX_MISS[UT],0)}% / {m6(CTX_MISS[SF],0)}%; attribution accuracy when decided above 99%", f"{PEA(UT,'D2','uncertain')}% and {PEA(SF,'D2','uncertain')}% of reported changes left uncertain; small increase in false escalation in utilities"],
              ["H3 cross-domain reliability", "Supported in a narrow sense", "dynamic estimator better than static and domain-specific baselines at matched coverage in both domains", f"simple change processes, independent elements, synthetic scaffold criterion (conclusions held in {SDN[('nominal','S1')]['n_dom']} of 16 alternative definitions of an unsafe structure, Section 8.4); gate benefits come mainly from abstention; point sensors and placement added little"]],
        widths=[1.4, 1.1, 2.6, 1.8], size=7.4)
